  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "Triangle \\(ABC\\) has a right angle at \\(B,\\) \\(AB=1,\\) and \\(BC=2.\\) The bisector of \\(\\angle BAC\\) meets \\(\\overline{BC}\\) at \\(D.\\) What is \\(BD?\\)",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt3-1}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt5+1}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt6+\\sqrt2}{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(2\\sqrt3-1\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10b-2009-p20.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "By the Pythagorean Theorem, \\(AC=\\sqrt{1^2+2^2}=\\sqrt5.\\) The Angle Bisector Theorem gives \\[ \\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\\dfrac{AB}{AC}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}, \\] so \\(DC=\\sqrt5\\,BD.\\)\n\nSince \\(BD+DC=2,\\) we have \\(BD(1+\\sqrt5)=2,\\) so \\[ BD=\\dfrac{2}{1+\\sqrt5}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt5-1}{2}. \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "By the Angle Bisector Theorem, \\(\\dfrac{BD}{DC}=\\dfrac{AB}{AC}\\)",
        "\\(AC=\\sqrt5\\) and \\(BD+DC=2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
